Polynomial derivatives function in C++ (35 points) One convenient way to represent a polynomial is by using a list of coefficients.
For example, the polynomial of degree 4 in x defined by P1(x) = 2.3x4 + 1.2x2 - 9.3x + 8.4 can be represented with five floating-point values stored 
in a vector: {2.3, 0.0, 1.2, -9.3, 8.4}.//It is important to notice the ordering of the coefficients and to explicitly include zero values for missing terms
to ensure that the position in the vector implicitly indicates the power of associated with each coefficient. 
Write a function named takePolyDerivative() that takes a reference to a vector of floating-point coefficients as an argument. 
The function alters the vector object so that it represents the polynomial resulting from taking the derivative of the input (once, with respect to the sole variable). 
The function return should be void. Don't return a new vector, but modify the one passed as input. Note that the derivative of 0 is 0. For example,
assume the input vector is {2.3, 0.0, 1.2, -9.3, 8.4}. The function will modify this vector into {9.2, 0.0, 2.4, -9.3}.

#include <vector>
using namespace std;
void takePolyDerivative(vector<float>& coeffs) {
    
int n = coeffs.size();
    
    // If polynomial is constant or empty
    if (n <= 1) {
        coeffs.clear();
        coeffs.push_back(0.0f);
        return;
    }

    int power = n - 1;  // highest power
    
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        coeffs[i] = coeffs[i] * power;
        power--;
    }

    // Remove the last element (constant term)
    coeffs.pop_back();
}

